				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				404,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Vine): Add common RPC argument DTO module for IPC serialization\n\nIntroduces shared GlobPattern data structure in Mountain's RPC argument common module to standardize IPC communication patterns between core and sidecars. This foundational work:\n\n- Provides reusable DTO definitions for cross-component RPC commands\n- Enables consistent glob pattern handling in Cocoon<->Mountain IPC via Vine transport\n- Supports upcoming implementation of file system operations requiring pattern matching\n- Aligns with MVP Path A focus by enhancing Cocoon integration capabilities\n\nPart of ongoing effort to solidify Vine IPC layer (refs 546516f). Contributes to Land's extensibility goals by establishing shared communication contracts.",
			"SHA": "f7855913803b525f2b7f738f7c2124e56d5ea1c9"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2025-06-06T23:44:09Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				402,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Vine): Add RPC command argument DTO modules for Cocoon IPC\n\nIntroduces structured argument definitions for command execution RPC calls between Mountain and Cocoon sidecar. Creates `ExecuteCommandArgument` and `RegisterArgument` modules under Vine's IPC layer to standardize data transfer objects used in:\n- Command registration workflow via Track dispatcher\n- Execution request handling in Cocoon's gRPC server\n\nThese DTOs form the foundation for Mountain's native command system integration with VS Code extension APIs through Cocoon shims. The types will be used in Vine's JSON-over-stdio IPC to serialize/deserialize command metadata and parameters, enabling:\n1. Extension commands to be registered in Mountain's Track dispatcher\n2. UI-triggered commands to be routed through Cocoon's shim layer\n\nPart of ongoing MVP Path A implementation to establish basic extension host communication (Deliverable #5). Directly supports recent Vine server implementations in commits a0598bb through 4d6a528.",
			"SHA": "546516f8898a320506c5b977e47d906ac841648b"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2025-06-06T23:43:43Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				398,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Vine): Implement platform-agnostic gRPC server for Cocoon IPC\n\nAdds CocoonVineGrpcService class to handle bidirectional communication between Mountain (Rust) and Cocoon (Node.js) via Vine IPC layer. The server supports:\n- Processing Mountain requests through HandleRequest\n- Forwarding notifications via HandleNotification\n- Streaming binary RPC data using HandleRpcData\n- Operation cancellation through HandleCancel\n\nImplements platform-specific binding logic for Windows named pipes and Unix domain sockets, aligning with Land's cross-platform requirements. Integrates with cancellation token registry for proper lifecycle management and includes error handling for service initialization failures.\n\nThis forms the Node.js side of Vine's gRPC transport layer, enabling extension host communication as part of MVP Path A's Cocoon implementation. Works with recently added gRPC handlers (b10e0d3, af61b47) to complete the IPC foundation for VS Code extension compatibility.\n\nRefs #123",
			"SHA": "a0598bb37dd5a3886ef1789ae14e4a230f5ed217"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2025-06-06T23:43:28Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				397,
				1
			],
			"Message": "feat(Vine): Structure gRPC server modules for Cocoon communication\n\nIntroduces modular organization for Cocoon's gRPC server implementation within Mountain, aligning with MVP Path A requirements for bidirectional IPC via Vine. The new module structure separates:\n- Lifecycle management (Initialize/Shutdown)\n- Request/response handling\n- Binary data processing\n- Notification and cancellation flows\n\nThis foundational work enables Mountain to host the gRPC service endpoint that Cocoon extensions will communicate with, implementing the Vine IPC layer's server-side components. The modular approach supports future expansion of RPC methods while maintaining separation of concerns, critical for maintaining performance in Rust-native IPC handling.\n\nRefs #af61b47a92020af3875c432a86e283ab35f15ac6, #4b4cd9ad0006ad4974b1c42f1190e28171e687d5",
			"SHA": "b10e0d3618d671543c92bceefbd64737903061ad"
		},
